implementationPlan.note:
SCOPE NOTE FIRST: the locator landed as TWO focused modules, not one. `expansion-release-selector-scan.ts` was 274 lines, over the epic's per-file 250 target, so the single-row payload JUDGEMENT was split out into `expansion-release-selector-items.ts` (+ `.js` bridge). Epic rail 5 names exactly this remedy ("per-file violations remain real and are fixed by splitting the file"). Result: scan 166, items 136. Two owned paths beyond the plan's ten; the alternative was shipping a file over the stated target.

SCAN (`expansion-release-selector-scan.ts`, 166 lines):
- pages ONLY `FOUNDATION_CONTEXT_EVENT_TYPE` via `readEventsByTypeAfter`, PAGE_SIZE 100, cursor from `0n`, bounded by the EXTERNALLY supplied horizon;
- `wellShaped` re-checks the page contract before trusting it (`hasMore` boolean, `items` array, `items.length <= PAGE_SIZE`, `nextCursor` null-or-bigint) — a filtered scan has no positional contiguity, so the pager's contract IS the completeness proof;
- per row: `globalPosition` must be a bigint STRICTLY greater than the last, must not exceed the horizon, and `eventType` must match; a page that advanced nothing while claiming `hasMore` refuses; `nextCursor !== position` refuses;
- NO early return on a hit: a candidate is pushed and the walk continues, so a later retry becomes AMBIGUOUS instead of a wrong answer;
- a thrown pager refuses UNREADABLE carrying `DurableStoreError.code` when there is one, null otherwise.

ROW VERIFICATION (`verifyRow`): decode -> strict `readFoundationContextManifest` on the slot the record's OWN bytes name (canonical payload, aggregate identity, decision AND receipt all proven) -> `sameBytes` byte-compare of the paged payload against the strict read (`PAGED_ROW_DIVERGED`) -> `canonicalItems` -> only THEN `namesThisParent`, so an unrelated seal is skipped only after it has been fully verified.

ITEMS (`expansion-release-selector-items.ts`, 136 lines): `canonicalItems` requires newline-delimited canonical JSON, refuses an embedded NUL, refuses a non-object line, requires `kind==="MANDATORY"` and the item's own SECTION (graph/plan/objective/authority), and requires EXACTLY ONE of each of the four mandatory ids — missing and duplicate both answer `null`. `itemsAgree` cross-checks eleven edges: graph goalRef/revisionId/graphContentHash/graphEpoch/planHash, plan runId/graphRevisionRef/planHash, objective nodeKey, activation attemptRef/ownerSessionRef.

PRODUCTION DEFECT FIXED HERE: `itemsAgree` compared `plan["planHash"] === graph["planHash"]`. Those are two INDEPENDENT facts — the context matrix renders the graph item's hash from the graph revision (`foundation-context-matrix.ts:214`) and the plan item's from the approved plan (`:218`) — and the measured world has `graph.planHash=1111…1111` against `plan.planHash=00312cae…`. The check refused every real world. It now compares `plan["planHash"]` against a new server-derived `expected.approvedPlanHash` taken from `readApprovedPlan`, which is a STRONGER join than the one it replaced: the plan item is now pinned to the durable approved plan rather than to its neighbour.

Candidates are returned as an ARRAY: zero/one/many is the orchestrator's call, never row order's.

Focused suite green after the split: `Test Files 1 passed (1) / Tests 44 passed (44)`, EXIT 0.
